---
export const prerender = true;

import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import DocCallout from "../../../components/docs/DocCallout.astro";
import DocCode from "../../../components/docs/DocCode.astro";
import DocRelated from "../../../components/docs/DocRelated.astro";

const toc = [
  { title: "Overview", href: "#overview", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Availability", href: "#availability", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Creating a host", href: "#create", depth: 2 },
  { title: "As an Effect layer", href: "#layer", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Directory binding", href: "#directory", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Registering tools", href: "#tools", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Shared instance state", href: "#shared-state", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Embedded or client?", href: "#choosing", depth: 2 },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Embedded SDK"
  subtitle="Run the whole nikcli server in your own process — same routes, same handlers, no listener and no socket."
  description="Embedding nikcli in-process with @nikcli-ai/sdk-next: creating a host, the Effect layer, directory binding, registering tools, and the shared instance state caveat."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="overview" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      <code>@nikcli-ai/sdk-next</code> is an embedded host. It builds the same typed client as
      <a href="/docs/build/client">the network SDK</a>, but hands it a <code>fetch</code> that calls
      the server's request handler directly in memory. No port is bound, no socket is opened, and
      nothing leaves the process.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything else is unchanged: the same routing, the same middleware, the same handlers, codecs
      and errors. Code written against the network client works against the embedded host without
      edits — which is the point.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="availability" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Availability</h2>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="In-repo today, not on npm">
      <p>
        <code>@nikcli-ai/sdk-next</code> is a workspace package and is not published. It is usable
        from inside the monorepo — and it is the migration target for the generated
        <code>@nikcli-ai/sdk</code> — but an external project cannot install it yet. If you are
        building outside this repository, take <a href="/docs/build/client">the client path</a>.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
    <p>
      It also depends on <code>nikcli-ai</code> itself rather than on a contract package, because
      embedding means running the real server: the whole agent is in your process, not a stub of it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="create" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Creating a host</h2>
    <p>
      <code>NikCli.create</code> returns the client surface, so the call sites look like the network
      client with an <code>Effect</code> wrapper around construction.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`import { NikCli } from "@nikcli-ai/sdk-next"
import { Effect } from "effect"

const program = Effect.gen(function* () {
  const nikcli = yield* NikCli.create({ directory: process.cwd() })

  const session = yield* Effect.promise(() =>
    nikcli.session.create({ title: "embedded" }),
  )

  return yield* Effect.promise(() =>
    nikcli.session.prompt({
      sessionID: session.data!.id,
      parts: [{ type: "text", text: "What changed in the last commit?" }],
    }),
  )
})`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      The result envelope is the same one the network client uses — calls resolve with
      <code>{"{ data, error }"}</code> rather than rejecting. See
      <a href="/docs/build/client#results">The result envelope</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="layer" class="doc-section">
    <h2>As an Effect layer</h2>
    <p>
      The same constructor is available as a service, for code that would rather take the host as a
      dependency than build one.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`import { NikCli } from "@nikcli-ai/sdk-next"
import { Effect } from "effect"

const program = Effect.gen(function* () {
  const nikcli = yield* NikCli.Service
  return yield* Effect.promise(() => nikcli.session.list())
})

await Effect.runPromise(program.pipe(Effect.provide(NikCli.layer)))`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="note" title="The layer is not a second implementation">
      <p>
        <code>NikCli.layer</code> only adapts <code>NikCli.create()</code> for dependency injection.
        There is one host, reachable two ways.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="directory" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Directory binding</h2>
    <p>
      The host binds requests to its <code>directory</code> — defaulting to
      <code>process.cwd()</code> — by setting <code>x-nikcli-directory</code> on any call that does
      not already carry its own <code>directory</code> query parameter or header. From there the
      request flows through the same per-request instance middleware as network traffic.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`const nikcli = yield* NikCli.create({ directory: "/Users/me/project" })

// bound to the host directory
yield* Effect.promise(() => nikcli.session.list())

// explicit selection still wins
yield* Effect.promise(() => nikcli.session.list({ directory: "/Users/me/other" }))`}</DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="tools" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Registering tools</h2>
    <p>
      The embedded host can do one thing the network client cannot: register tools directly into the
      instance's <code>ToolRegistry</code> — the same registry sessions consult and
      <code>GET /experimental/tool/ids</code> reports.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`import { NikCli, Tool } from "@nikcli-ai/sdk-next"

const program = Effect.gen(function* () {
  const nikcli = yield* NikCli.create({ directory: process.cwd() })
  yield* nikcli.tools.register(MyTool)
})`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Why this is local-only">
      <p>
        A tool is executable code. It cannot travel over HTTP, so there is no endpoint that could
        offer this and no way to do it from the network client. Registration is the reason to embed
        rather than connect.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="shared-state" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Shared instance state</h2>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Two hosts in one process are not isolated">
      <p>
        Unlike a network server, the embedded host shares process-global instance state. Two
        <code>create()</code> calls in the same process observe the same projects, sessions and
        registered tools for a given directory. Treat the host as a process-wide singleton; if you
        need isolation, you need separate processes.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="choosing" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Embedded or client?</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Embedded</th><th>Client</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Package</td><td><code>@nikcli-ai/sdk-next</code> (in-repo)</td><td><code>@nikcli-ai/sdk</code> (npm)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Transport</td><td>In-memory handler call</td><td>HTTP</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Needs a running server</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Register tools in code</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cross-process / cross-machine</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Isolation between hosts</td><td>None — process-global</td><td>Per server</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Auth</td><td>Not applicable</td><td>Bearer, basic, Tailscale</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      If your program is a script, a bot or a separate interface, connect. If your program
      <em>is</em> the product and nikcli is a component of it, embed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <DocRelated
    links={[
      { title: "Build", href: "/docs/build", description: "The three ways to build on nikcli, and which to pick." },
      { title: "Client", href: "/docs/build/client", description: "The same surface over HTTP, installable from npm." },
      { title: "API Reference", href: "/docs/api", description: "The endpoints the embedded host serves in memory." },
      { title: "Tools", href: "/docs/tools", description: "The built-in tools a registered tool sits beside." },
    ]}
  />
</DocsLayout>
